			],
			"Message": "feat(Wind/Integration): Replace Tauri fs plugin with Mountain backend file operations\n\nThis commit completes Wind's transition from direct filesystem API usage to full backend abstraction by replacing the Tauri fs plugin with Mountain's unified IPC interface for all file operations. This aligns with Land's architectural principle of delegating all privileged operations to the native Mountain backend through structured communication channels.\n\nKey changes include:\n- Refactored FileService to use `mountain_ipc_invoke` commands (`file:read`, `file:write`, `file:stat`, etc.) instead of direct Tauri fs plugin imports\n- Enhanced MountainIntegrationService with actual gRPC call implementation via `mountain_grpc_call` command, replacing simulation logic\n- Added comprehensive test suites for file operations and Mountain integration, ensuring robust error handling and type safety\n- Updated configuration sync to include client identification and sync metadata for better observability\n\nThese changes strengthen Wind's production readiness by establishing real communication with Mountain for filesystem operations, enabling centralized file management, enhanced security through backend delegation, and improved monitoring capabilities across the Land ecosystem.",
			"SHA": "c3042d3d1a625c53129373defd19a219f682418c"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-01-30T10:26:41Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				399,
				6
			],
			"Message": "feat(Wind/Integration): Implement Mountain health monitoring service and refine IPC abstraction\n\nThis commit establishes Wind's comprehensive health monitoring system for real-time observation of Mountain backend services, advancing Land's commitment to robust, observable cross-platform architecture. The HealthService provides proactive health tracking, service discovery, and automatic recovery capabilities through structured event-driven communication over Tauri's IPC boundary.\n\nKey additions include the new HealthService in the Bootstrap layer, which monitors Mountain services via dedicated Tauri commands (`mountain_get_services_status`, `mountain_restart_service`) and events (`mountain_service_status`, `mountain_health_alert`). The service implements configurable heartbeat monitoring, error threshold detection, and exponential backoff recovery attempts, reflecting Land's effects-based approach to side effect management.\n\nConcurrently, TauriFileService was refactored to use abstracted `mountain_ipc_invoke` commands instead of direct Tauri filesystem APIs, strengthening the separation between Wind's service layer and Mountain's native implementation. Configuration service validation was enhanced with type-safe key/value checking, and import statements were optimized across integration points for better code organization.\n\nThese changes significantly improve Wind's production readiness by enabling real-time health visibility and reinforcing the declarative IPC boundary between the TypeScript frontend services and Rust backend.",
			"SHA": "b14a288ff1f59a9e24403e49841b3fcc0114927a"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-01-30T10:15:49Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				398,
				3
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Wind/Integration): Enhance configuration service resiliency and remove API mocks\n\nThis commit significantly improves the robustness of Wind's configuration synchronization with the Mountain backend by implementing proper error handling and removing development mocks.\n\nIn TauriConfigurationService, added graceful fallback to default configuration when loading fails, ensuring the application remains usable even with configuration file corruption. Implemented exponential backoff retry logic with conflict resolution for configuration saves, reflecting Land's commitment to reliable state synchronization across the Tauri IPC boundary.\n\nRemoved mock implementations from MountainWindSync and replaced them with real Tauri API imports (@tauri-apps/api/core), marking a key milestone in the Wind service layer's transition from development to production readiness. These changes ensure Wind can now establish real bidirectional communication with the Mountain backend via Tauri commands and events.\n\nThe simplied import in TauriIPCServer demonstrates improved code organization as the Wind services mature toward the final Effect-TS based architecture.",
			"SHA": "aef03aed85c8d92e0ffe79e3f270f911491ee035"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-01-30T08:27:19Z",
